#pagebreak()
= R and ggplot2

== Question and data

Do the four series have similar least-squares summaries despite different
geometries? Read the same 44 observations from the versioned CSV.

```{r}
#| show: both
#| depends: [data/anscombe.csv]
points <- read.csv("data/anscombe.csv")
```

== Calculation

Estimate a line separately for each series, using sample variances with divisor
$n-1$. Export the unrounded summary for Typst and cross-language checks.

```{r}
#| show: none
summaries <- do.call(rbind, lapply(split(points, points$series), function(d) {
  model <- lm(y ~ x, data = d)
  data.frame(series = d$series[1], n = nrow(d),
    mean_x = mean(d$x), mean_y = mean(d$y),
    var_x = var(d$x), var_y = var(d$y),
    slope = unname(coef(model)[2]), intercept = unname(coef(model)[1]),
    r_squared = summary(model)$r.squared)
}))
lines <- do.call(rbind, lapply(seq_len(nrow(summaries)), function(i) {
  s <- summaries[i, ]
  data.frame(series = s$series, x = c(3, 20),
    y = s$intercept + s$slope * c(3, 20))
}))
export_data(summaries, "r-summary")
```

== Numerical summary

#if "r-summary" in knot-data {
  results-table(knot-data.at("r-summary"))
} else { [Results pending or unavailable.] }

There are `{r} nrow(points)` observations. The means and fitted coefficients
are almost the same across the four series. Inspecting only this table would
hide the main differences.

== Graphical check

The panels share their scales; the grey lines are the precomputed least-squares
fits, not new calculations performed by the graphics package.

```{r}
library(ggplot2)
plot <- ggplot(points, aes(x, y)) +
  geom_line(data = lines, colour = "#64748b", linewidth = 0.6) +
  geom_point(colour = "#176b87", size = 2) +
  facet_wrap(~series, ncol = 2) +
  coord_cartesian(xlim = c(3, 20), ylim = c(2, 14)) +
  labs(x = "x", y = "y") + theme_minimal(base_size = 11)
typst(plot)
```

